1	// The executor's entry gate: identify the ONE dispatch this session was started
2	// for, and validate it in code BEFORE any model judgment (per-project-scheduling
3	// DESIGN §5.2). This is the CLI shell `validate-dispatch.mjs` was written to be
4	// driven by — it wires that pure core's `exists` / `isPackDeclared` / `loadTask`
5	// capabilities to this checkout and hands it the issue body.
6	//
7	// IT DOES NOT CLAIM. The claim protocol (read labels → swap ready → agent-running
8	// → post a claim comment → re-read, earliest claim wins) needs GitHub WRITES,
9	// which the executor session can only make through its MCP tools, so it stays
10	// agent-driven prose in executor.md. This shell only decides "is there a dispatch
11	// here, is it mine, and is it legal".
12	//
13	// TWO TRIGGER SOURCES, because the same `issues.labeled` webhook reaches an
14	// executor session by two different transports and only one of them was ever
15	// read here:
16	//
17	//   1. GITHUB ACTIONS writes the whole webhook payload to disk at
18	//      `$GITHUB_EVENT_PATH` — `action`, `label.name` and the entire `issue`
19	//      object, `issue.number` and `issue.body` included. Payload plus local
20	//      checkout is everything the validation needs, in one shot.
21	//   2. CLAUDE CODE ON THE WEB (CCR) delivers the same trigger as environment
22	//      variables instead — `CCR_TRIGGER_SOURCE`, `CCR_TRIGGER_EVENT`,
23	//      `CCR_TRIGGER_REPO`, `CCR_TRIGGER_ISSUE_NUMBER` — and writes no payload
24	//      file at all. It NAMES the issue but carries neither the label that was
25	//      added nor the body, so it resolves in two shots: this shell reports
26	//      `needs-issue` with the number, the executor fetches that one issue over
27	//      MCP, saves the tool's raw JSON response verbatim to a file, and
28	//      re-invokes with `--issue-json` for the identical validation. The shell
29	//      extracts body, labels, and title from the response itself — the agent
30	//      pastes bytes, it never hand-extracts fields — and rejects a response
31	//      whose `number` is not the trigger's, so fetching the wrong issue is
32	//      caught in code. (`--issue-body-file` + `--issue-labels` remain as the
33	//      manual fallback should the fetch's response shape ever surprise.)
34	//
35	// Reading only source 1 is what made every CCR-run executor session miss its own
36	// trigger and select an issue by listing instead — the duplicate-execution bug
37	// re-entered through the front door. Observed live 2026-07-28: two sessions each
38	// selected dispatch #772 and claimed it one second apart.
39	//
40	// ZERO NETWORK, BY CONSTRUCTION — still. The executor session is MCP-only and
41	// carries no repo credential of its own, so anything reaching the GitHub REST API
42	// here would both fail to authenticate and trip the in-session-github-access
43	// rule. The CCR path does not change that: this shell never fetches the issue
44	// itself, it tells the EXECUTOR to fetch it over MCP and hand the bytes back.
45	//
46	// THE VERDICT IS THE INTERFACE; THE EXIT CODE IS ONLY "did the routine break".
47	// The executor branches on the printed `dispatch:` field, which every decided
48	// verdict carries. The exit code answers a narrower question, and answers it the
49	// way every other command does (owner, 2026-08-14): ZERO WHEN THE ROUTINE GOES
50	// ON — including when going on means stopping on purpose — and NON-ZERO ONLY
51	// WHEN IT STOPS UNEXPECTEDLY. A verdict this shell was written to reach is not a
52	// failure, and a harness that paints exit 11 red teaches both the reader and the
53	// agent to read an ordinary "not mine" as a fault.
54	//
55	// Exit 0, `dispatch:` names which one:
56	//
57	//   valid       — a legal dispatch for this session's scope; go claim it.
58	//   needs-issue — a CCR trigger named the issue but carries no body/labels.
59	//                 Fetch THAT issue over MCP and re-invoke with them. The routine
60	//                 is mid-handshake, not stopped.
61	//   invalid     — a forged or mangled dispatch. Comment the printed `reason`,
62	//                 remove the ready label, add `needs-human`, end the session.
63	//                 It never runs. Prescribed work, so: zero.
64	//   task-gone   — a well-formed dispatch naming a task this repo no longer
65	//                 carries (file removed, pack undeclared). CLOSE the issue
66	//                 with the printed reason (owner, 2026-08-06) — an obsolete
67	//                 dispatch is not a human's problem. It never runs.
68	//   not-mine    — the trigger label is no ready label at all, or the issue no
69	//                 longer carries one (a dispatch another session has already
70	//                 claimed). Stop. Change nothing, comment nothing. An expected
71	//                 stop is not an error.
72	//
73	// Non-zero — the routine stops and something needs a human:
74	//
75	//   12 no-trigger      — NO source names an issue. STOP THE SESSION. There is no
76	//                        fallback: never select a dispatch by listing (see below).
77	//   15 scope-mismatch  — the trigger label is the OTHER executor's ready label.
78	//                        Each routine fires on its own label, so this is a
79	//                        MISCONFIGURED ROUTINE (a fleet routine whose prompt lost
80	//                        the word `fleet`, most often), not a dispatch to adopt.
81	//                        Stop — change nothing, comment nothing — but stop LOUDLY:
82	//                        nothing on GitHub records this, the janitor re-arms the
83	//                        dispatch, and it declines forever until a human reads it.
84	//   2  usage           — bad invocation (an unknown scope argument, an unreadable
85	//                        `--issue-body-file`).
86	//   1  internal        — an unexpected fault in this shell.
87	//
88	// Codes 10, 11, 13 and 14 are RETIRED, not renumbered — every one of them now
89	// exits 0 under its own `dispatch:` name, and no live code carries a changed
90	// meaning for a reader who remembers the old table.
91	//
92	// THERE IS NO FALLBACK, BY DESIGN. Exit 12 used to send the executor off to list
93	// the open dispatches and take the oldest. That is precisely the
94	// N-sessions-racing-over-N-issues failure the one-session-one-issue rule exists to
95	// prevent, reached from the other direction: one scheduler run files every due
96	// dispatch seconds apart, so every session that cannot name its own trigger builds
97	// the SAME work list and races over it. A session that does not know its issue
98	// must run nothing — the daily task-janitor re-arms an unrun dispatch,
99	// so stopping costs a delay while guessing costs duplicated work.
100	//
101	// Usage: `node <engine>/scheduler/resolve-dispatch.mjs [self|fleet]`
102	//                `[--issue-json <path> | --issue-body-file <path> --issue-labels <csv>]`
103	// The positional argument is THIS SESSION's scope — which of the two executor
104	// routines is running. It defaults to `self`, which is every ordinary project's
105	// executor; the FLEET routine must pass `fleet` explicitly, and a fleet payload
106	// arriving at a session that did not is reported as not-mine with that stated
107	// plainly. The flags carry what a CCR trigger cannot, and are ignored on the
108	// Actions path (whose payload already has everything).
109	
110	import { existsSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
111	import { dirname, join, sep } from 'node:path';
112	import { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';
113	import { DISPATCH_PATH_RE, dispatchFirstLine, validateDispatchBody } from './validate-dispatch.mjs';
114	import { parseDispatchTitle, readyLabelForScope } from './dispatch.mjs';
115	import { renderTaskExec } from './run-record.mjs';
116	import { SESSION_SCOPES } from './task-contract.mjs';
117	import { SHARED_SUBDIR } from '../pack_loader/pack-registry.mjs';
118	
119	export const EXIT = {
120	  ok: 0,
121	  internal: 1,
122	  usage: 2,
123	  noTrigger: 12,
124	  scopeMismatch: 15,
125	};
126	
127	// The executor scope a ready label implies — the exact inverse of the mapping the
128	// SCHEDULER files a dispatch under (`readyLabelForScope`), derived from it rather
129	// than restated, so the two can never drift. `null` = not a ready label at all.
130	export const scopeForLabel = (label) =>
131	  SESSION_SCOPES.find((scope) => readyLabelForScope(scope) === label) ?? null;
132	
133	// Which checkout do the task paths in a dispatch body resolve against? Answered
134	// from where THIS engine copy is mounted, not from cwd — a consumer runs the
135	// vendored engine at `<root>/.claudinite/shared/engine/scheduler/`, the canon
136	// repo runs its own at `<root>/engine/scheduler/`. Deriving it from the module's
137	// own location means whichever copy the executor invoked resolves against that
138	// copy's own repo, with nothing to pass — and nothing for executor.md to explain.
139	const MOUNT_SUFFIX = sep + SHARED_SUBDIR;
140	export function repoRootFrom(moduleUrl) {
141	  const home = dirname(dirname(dirname(fileURLToPath(moduleUrl)))); // <home>/engine/scheduler/<this file>
142	  return home.endsWith(MOUNT_SUFFIX) ? home.slice(0, -MOUNT_SUFFIX.length) : home;
143	}
144	
145	// Read the webhook payload the label event was started with. Every way of not
146	// having one collapses to a single `{ error }` — unset, unreadable, unparsable —
147	// because the caller's response to all of them is the same: try the other
148	// trigger source, and stop the session if that names no issue either.
149	export function readEventPayload(env = process.env, read = (p) => readFileSync(p, 'utf8')) {
150	  const path = env.GITHUB_EVENT_PATH;
151	  if (!path) return { error: 'GITHUB_EVENT_PATH is not set — this session has no event payload on disk' };
152	  let raw;
153	  try {
154	    raw = read(path);
155	  } catch (e) {
156	    return { error: `GITHUB_EVENT_PATH points at ${path}, which is unreadable: ${e.message}` };
157	  }
158	  let event;
159	  try {
160	    event = JSON.parse(raw);
161	  } catch (e) {
162	    return { error: `the event payload at ${path} is not valid JSON: ${e.message}` };
163	  }
164	  if (event === null || typeof event !== 'object' || Array.isArray(event)) {
165	    return { error: `the event payload at ${path} is not an event object` };
166	  }
167	  return { event };
168	}
169	
170	// The three facts a dispatch trigger must carry. Anything short of all three
171	// means the payload cannot name this session's issue — not a rejection of a
172	// dispatch, just this source failing to identify one; the caller tries the other
173	// source before giving up.
174	export function triggerFromEvent(event) {
175	  const action = event.action;
176	  const label = event.label?.name;
177	  const number = event.issue?.number;
178	  if (action !== 'labeled') return { error: `the event payload is a "${action}" event, not a label event — it names no dispatch` };
179	  if (typeof label !== 'string' || label === '') return { error: 'the label event carries no label.name' };
180	  if (!Number.isInteger(number)) return { error: 'the label event carries no issue.number' };
181	  return { trigger: { source: 'payload', label, number, body: event.issue?.body ?? '', title: event.issue?.title ?? '' } };
182	}
183	
184	// The CCR trigger: same webhook, delivered as environment variables by Claude
185	// Code on the web, which writes no payload file. It names the issue and nothing
186	// else — no `label.name`, no `issue.body` — so the trigger it yields is
187	// deliberately PARTIAL, and `main` turns that into the two-shot `needs-issue`
188	// handshake rather than guessing either missing field.
189	export function triggerFromCcrEnv(env = process.env) {
190	  const source = env.CCR_TRIGGER_SOURCE;
191	  const event = env.CCR_TRIGGER_EVENT;
192	  const raw = env.CCR_TRIGGER_ISSUE_NUMBER;
193	  if (!source && !event && !raw) return { error: 'no CCR_TRIGGER_* variables are set either — this session carries no CCR trigger' };
194	  if (source !== 'github') return { error: `CCR_TRIGGER_SOURCE is ${JSON.stringify(source ?? null)}, not "github" — this session was not started by a GitHub event` };
195	  if (event !== 'issues.labeled') return { error: `CCR_TRIGGER_EVENT is ${JSON.stringify(event ?? null)}, not "issues.labeled" — it names no dispatch` };
196	  const number = Number(raw);
197	  if (!raw || !Number.isInteger(number) || number <= 0) return { error: `CCR_TRIGGER_ISSUE_NUMBER is ${JSON.stringify(raw ?? null)}, which is not an issue number` };
198	  return { trigger: { source: 'ccr', number, repo: env.CCR_TRIGGER_REPO ?? '' } };
199	}
200	
201	// Identify this session's ONE dispatch from whichever transport carried it.
202	// Order is preference, not precedence-by-truth: the Actions payload is tried
203	// first only because it answers in one shot, and a session that somehow has both
204	// gets the same issue from either. Every failure of both sources is collected
205	// into one reason, because the executor prints it and then stops.
206	export function resolveTrigger(env = process.env, read) {
207	  const reasons = [];
208	  const { event, error: payloadError } = readEventPayload(env, read);
209	  if (payloadError) reasons.push(payloadError);
210	  else {
211	    const { trigger, error } = triggerFromEvent(event);
212	    if (trigger) return { trigger };
213	    reasons.push(error);
214	  }
215	  const { trigger: ccr, error: ccrError } = triggerFromCcrEnv(env);
216	  if (ccr) return { trigger: ccr };
217	  reasons.push(ccrError);
218	  return { error: reasons.join('; ') };
219	}
220	
221	// `[scope] [--flag value|--flag=value]`. Kept deliberately tiny — the shell takes
222	// one positional and two flags, and a dependency-free parser is less surface than
223	// the alternative.
224	export function parseArgs(argv) {
225	  const positional = [];
226	  const flags = {};
227	  for (let i = 0; i < argv.length; i++) {
228	    const arg = argv[i];
229	    if (!arg.startsWith('--')) { positional.push(arg); continue; }
230	    const eq = arg.indexOf('=');
231	    if (eq !== -1) flags[arg.slice(2, eq)] = arg.slice(eq + 1);
232	    else flags[arg.slice(2)] = argv[++i] ?? '';
233	  }
234	  return { positional, flags };
235	}
236	
237	// Which of the issue's CURRENT labels is a ready label? On the CCR path this
238	// stands in for the `label.name` the trigger never carried — and it is strictly
239	// more informative than the event's: a dispatch another session already claimed
240	// no longer carries a ready label at all, so this catches the lost race here at
241	// step 1 rather than at the claim.
242	export function readyLabelAmong(labels) {
243	  const ready = labels.filter((l) => scopeForLabel(l) !== null);
244	  if (ready.length === 0) return { error: `it carries no ready label (it has: ${labels.join(', ') || 'none'}) — its dispatch has already been claimed or converged by another session` };
245	  if (ready.length > 1) return { error: `it carries more than one ready label (${ready.join(', ')}), so which executor owns it is ambiguous` };
246	  return { label: ready[0] };
247	}
248	
249	// The CCR handshake's payload: the raw issue-fetch response, saved verbatim.
250	// The whole point is that the executor pastes bytes instead of hand-extracting
251	// fields — a body threaded through the shell mangles quoting, a hand-assembled
252	// labels CSV drops one. Accepts both label shapes (the MCP tools return plain
253	// strings, the REST API returns { name } objects), and surfaces `number` so the
254	// caller can assert the agent fetched the issue the trigger actually names.
255	export function parseIssueJson(raw) {
256	  let issue;
257	  try {
258	    issue = JSON.parse(raw);
259	  } catch (e) {
260	    return { error: `not valid JSON: ${e.message}` };
261	  }
262	  if (issue === null || typeof issue !== 'object' || Array.isArray(issue)) {
263	    return { error: 'not an issue object' };
264	  }
265	  const labels = (Array.isArray(issue.labels) ? issue.labels : [])
266	    .map((l) => (typeof l === 'string' ? l : l?.name))
267	    .filter((l) => typeof l === 'string' && l !== '');
268	  return { issue: {
269	    number: Number.isInteger(issue.number) ? issue.number : null,
270	    body: typeof issue.body === 'string' ? issue.body : '',
271	    labels,
272	    title: typeof issue.title === 'string' ? issue.title : '',
273	  } };
274	}
275	
276	// The block the executor reads. `key: value` lines, one fact per line: an agent
277	// quoting a field back must not have to parse prose, and a reader diffing two
278	// runs must see exactly what changed.
279	const block = (fields) => Object.entries(fields).map(([k, v]) => `${k}: ${v}`).join('\n');
280	
281	function done(code, fields, advice) {
282	  console.log(block(fields));
283	  if (advice) console.error(`resolve-dispatch: ${advice}`);
284	  process.exit(code);
285	}
286	
287	async function main() {
288	  const { positional, flags } = parseArgs(process.argv.slice(2));
289	  const scopeGiven = positional.length > 0;
290	  const scope = positional[0] ?? 'self';
291	  if (!SESSION_SCOPES.includes(scope)) {
292	    console.error(`resolve-dispatch: unknown scope "${scope}" — usage: node resolve-dispatch.mjs [${SESSION_SCOPES.join('|')}] [--issue-json <path> | --issue-body-file <path> --issue-labels <csv>]`);
293	    process.exit(EXIT.usage);
294	  }
295	
296	  const { trigger, error: triggerError } = resolveTrigger();
297	  if (triggerError) {
298	    done(EXIT.noTrigger, { dispatch: 'no-trigger', scope, reason: triggerError },
299	      `${triggerError}. No trigger source names an issue, so this session cannot know which dispatch it was started for. STOP: run nothing, change nothing, comment nothing, end the session. There is NO fallback — never pick an issue by listing ${readyLabelForScope(scope)}; every dispatch in that list already has its own session, and the task-janitor re-arms an unrun one on its next daily run.`);
300	  }
301	
302	  let { label, number, body } = trigger;
303	  let title = trigger.title ?? '';
304	
305	  // The CCR handshake: the trigger named the issue, the executor fetched it over
306	  // MCP, and hands back here — as the raw response, or the manual two-flag
307	  // fallback — the fields the environment could not carry.
308	  if (trigger.source === 'ccr') {
309	    const jsonFile = flags['issue-json'];
310	    const bodyFile = flags['issue-body-file'];
311	    const labelsCsv = flags['issue-labels'];
312	    let labels;
313	    if (jsonFile !== undefined) {
314	      let raw;
315	      try {
316	        raw = readFileSync(jsonFile, 'utf8');
317	      } catch (e) {
318	        console.error(`resolve-dispatch: --issue-json ${jsonFile} is unreadable: ${e.message}`);
319	        process.exit(EXIT.usage);
320	      }
321	      const { issue, error } = parseIssueJson(raw);
322	      if (error) {
323	        console.error(`resolve-dispatch: --issue-json ${jsonFile} is ${error}`);
324	        process.exit(EXIT.usage);
325	      }
326	      if (issue.number !== null && issue.number !== number) {
327	        console.error(`resolve-dispatch: --issue-json carries issue #${issue.number}, but this session's trigger names #${number} — that is the wrong issue. Fetch #${number} alone and re-run.`);
328	        process.exit(EXIT.usage);
329	      }
330	      ({ body, title } = issue);
331	      labels = issue.labels;
332	    } else if (bodyFile !== undefined && labelsCsv !== undefined) {
333	      try {
334	        body = readFileSync(bodyFile, 'utf8');
335	      } catch (e) {
336	        console.error(`resolve-dispatch: --issue-body-file ${bodyFile} is unreadable: ${e.message}`);
337	        process.exit(EXIT.usage);
338	      }
339	      labels = labelsCsv.split(',').map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);
340	    } else {
341	      done(EXIT.ok, { dispatch: 'needs-issue', issue: number, scope, source: 'ccr', repo: trigger.repo || '(unset)' },
342	        `the CCR trigger names issue #${number} but carries neither its body nor its labels. Fetch ISSUE #${number} ALONE over MCP (the issue-get tool), save the tool's raw JSON response verbatim to a file, then re-run: node <engine>/scheduler/resolve-dispatch.mjs ${scope} --issue-json <path>. Do not list, select, or touch any other issue.`);
343	    }
344	    const { label: ready, error: labelError } = readyLabelAmong(labels);
345	    if (labelError) {
346	      done(EXIT.ok, { dispatch: 'not-mine', issue: number, scope, labels: labels.join('|') || '(none)' },
347	        `issue #${number}: ${labelError}. Stop: change nothing, comment nothing.`);
348	    }
349	    label = ready;
350	  }
351	
352	  const labelScope = scopeForLabel(label);
353	  if (labelScope === null) {
354	    done(EXIT.ok, { dispatch: 'not-mine', issue: number, scope, label },
355	      `issue #${number} was labeled "${label}", which is not a ready label — this is not an executor dispatch. Stop: change nothing, comment nothing.`);
356	  }
357	  // The one stop that is NOT ordinary. Each executor routine fires on its own
358	  // ready label, so a dispatch arriving under the other scope's label did not
359	  // wander in — the routine that woke for it is misconfigured, and nothing on
360	  // GitHub will ever say so. Hence non-zero: the session is the only place a
361	  // human can read it, and a green exit there reads as "nothing to see".
362	  if (labelScope !== scope) {
363	    done(EXIT.scopeMismatch, { dispatch: 'scope-mismatch', issue: number, scope, label, labelScope },
364	      `issue #${number} is labeled "${label}", a ${labelScope}-scoped dispatch, but this session's scope is "${scope}"${scopeGiven ? '' : ' (the default — pass "fleet" if this IS the fleet executor)'}. Each routine fires on its own ready label, so this session's routine is misconfigured — it will decline every ${labelScope} dispatch until a human fixes its launcher prompt. Stop: change nothing, comment nothing, and say so plainly in your final message.`);
365	  }
366	
367	  // The checkout the dispatch's task path must resolve in. `exists` reads the
368	  // working tree; in an executor session that IS HEAD (a fresh checkout, nothing
369	  // written yet), which is what validate-dispatch means by "exists at HEAD".
370	  const root = process.env.CLAUDINITE_REPO_ROOT || repoRootFrom(import.meta.url);
371	  const { loadConfig } = await import('../checks/helpers/repo-context.mjs');
372	  const declared = new Set(loadConfig(root).packs);
373	
374	  // `loadTask` is SYNCHRONOUS by design — it keeps validate-dispatch's core pure
375	  // and sync-testable — but importing an .mjs is not, so the shell prefetches the
376	  // module here and the capability just replays the result (or rethrows the parse
377	  // failure, which is exactly what the core wants to report).
378	  const firstLine = dispatchFirstLine(body);
379	  const mjsRelative = firstLine.replace(/task\.md$/, 'task.mjs');
380	  let loaded = null;
381	  let loadError = null;
382	  if (DISPATCH_PATH_RE.test(firstLine) && existsSync(join(root, mjsRelative))) {
383	    try {
384	      loaded = (await import(pathToFileURL(join(root, mjsRelative)).href)).default;
385	    } catch (e) {
386	      loadError = e;
387	    }
388	  }
389	
390	  const verdict = validateDispatchBody(body, {
391	    exists: (p) => existsSync(join(root, p)),
392	    isPackDeclared: (id) => declared.has(id),
393	    loadTask: () => { if (loadError) throw loadError; return loaded; },
394	  });
395	
396	  const slotForRecord = parseDispatchTitle(title)?.slotId ?? null;
397	  if (!verdict.ok) {
398	    // The execution record for a code-decided terminal verdict, printed HERE so
399	    // the count never depends on the agent remembering to run record-exec.
400	    const record = verdict.pack
401	      ? renderTaskExec({ pack: verdict.pack, task: verdict.task, slotId: slotForRecord, status: verdict.gone ? 'task-gone' : 'invalid' })
402	      : null;
403	    if (verdict.gone) {
404	      done(EXIT.ok, {
405	        dispatch: 'task-gone', issue: number, scope, label, reason: verdict.reason,
406	        ...(record ? { record } : {}),
407	      },
408	        `issue #${number} names a task this repo no longer carries: ${verdict.reason}. It must not run and needs no human: comment the reason, CLOSE the issue (not planned), and end the session. Do not add "needs-human".`);
409	    }
410	    done(EXIT.ok, {
411	      dispatch: 'invalid', issue: number, scope, label, reason: verdict.reason,
412	      ...(record ? { record } : {}),
413	    },
414	      `issue #${number} is not a valid dispatch: ${verdict.reason}. It must not run — comment naming what failed, remove the "${label}" label, add "needs-human", and end the session.`);
415	  }
416	
417	  // `brief` is the one line the executor quotes prominently in chat before it
418	  // acts, so a human skimming the session sees at a glance which task ran and
419	  // under what parameters. The slot comes from the dispatch title when the
420	  // trigger carried one (the Actions payload and --issue-json both do; the
421	  // manual two-flag fallback does not).
422	  const slot = slotForRecord;
423	  done(EXIT.ok, {
424	    dispatch: 'valid',
425	    brief: `Task: ${verdict.pack}/${verdict.task}${slot ? ` (slot ${slot})` : ''} — issue #${number}, model ${verdict.resolvedModel}, outcome ceiling ${verdict.outcome}, timeout ${verdict.executionTimeout ?? 'none'}s`,
426	    issue: number,
427	    scope,
428	    label,
429	    source: trigger.source,
430	    taskPath: verdict.taskPath,
431	    pack: verdict.pack,
432	    task: verdict.task,
433	    slot: slot ?? 'unknown',
434	    model: verdict.model,
435	    resolvedModel: verdict.resolvedModel,
436	    outcome: verdict.outcome,
437	    executionTimeout: verdict.executionTimeout ?? 'none',
438	  });
439	}
440	
441	// Run only when invoked directly (the executor's `node resolve-dispatch.mjs`),
442	// never on import — the exported helpers above are unit-testable without it.
443	if (process.argv[1] && import.meta.url === pathToFileURL(process.argv[1]).href) {
444	  main().catch((e) => { console.error(`resolve-dispatch: ${e.stack || e}`); process.exit(EXIT.internal); });
445	}
446

